From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rosen Penev <rosenp@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 17:15:35 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: b43: HT-PHY: restore the right bbmult copy after sample
 playback

b43_phy_ht_run_samples() saves the baseband multiplier of each core,
table 13 entry 0x63 + 4 * core, and b43_phy_ht_stop_playback() writes it
back. The second copy of that value is at 0x73 + 4 * core, where
b43_phy_ht_tx_power_fix() writes it and where Broadcom's driver restores
it, but stop_playback wrote it to 0x67 + 4 * core, the first copy of the
next core. So stopping a playback set core 2 and 3 to the multiplier of
core 1 and 2.

The saved values also stayed valid after the restore. run_samples() only
saves when there is no saved value, so every later playback restored the
multiplier saved by the first one, however the TX gain had changed since.
Forget the saved value once it is restored.

Fixes: 371ec465a358 ("b43: HT-PHY: implement stopping sample tone playback")
Signed-off-by: Rosen Penev <rosenp@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_ht.c | 3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_ht.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_ht.c
@@ -386,8 +386,9 @@ static void b43_phy_ht_stop_playback(str
 		if (phy_ht->bb_mult_save[i] >= 0) {
 			b43_httab_write(dev, B43_HTTAB16(13, 0x63 + i * 4),
 					phy_ht->bb_mult_save[i]);
-			b43_httab_write(dev, B43_HTTAB16(13, 0x67 + i * 4),
+			b43_httab_write(dev, B43_HTTAB16(13, 0x73 + i * 4),
 					phy_ht->bb_mult_save[i]);
+			phy_ht->bb_mult_save[i] = -1;
 		}
 	}
 }
